told:_IntellectualProperty_50
	a gist:IntellectualProperty ;
	skos:prefLabel "Tibetan Trade Delegation to the US and  UK, 1948"@en ;
	gist:description """<p>
	The Tibetan Trade Delegation to the US and the UK stand outside 10 Downing Street with Prime Minister Clement Atlee. The photo shows, from left to right, Khenchung Tubten Tsepel Taikhang (younger brother of Shakabpa), Finance Minister Wangchuk Deden Shakabpa, Yampel Pangdatsang, Prime Minister Clement Atlee, Surkhang Lhawang Topgyal and Kaludharma Ratna (interpreter). An Advanced Political History of Tibet misidentifies Surkhang Lhawang Topgyal as his father Dzasa Surkhang Surpa Wangchen Tseten, but in fact it was Surkhang Lhawang Topgyal who was part of the trade delegation as its interpreter.&nbsp;</p>"""@en ;
	gist:isCategorizedBy tolx:_WorkType_photograph ;
	gist:isConnectedTo told:_HistoricalPeriod_79 ;
	gist:name "Tibetan Trade Delegation to the US and  UK, 1948"@en ;
	tolo:credit "One Hundred Thousand Moons: An Advanced Political History of Tibet by Tsepon Wangchuck Deden Shakabpa, translated by Derek Maher"^^xsd:string ;
	.

told:_IntellectualProperty_51
	a gist:IntellectualProperty ;
	skos:prefLabel "Padmasambhava"@en ;
	gist:description """<p>
	This eighteenth century painting features Padmasambhava surrounded by his consorts Yeshe Tsogyel and Mandarava. Below are Śāntarakṣita&nbsp; and Trisong Detsen.&nbsp;</p>"""@en ;
	gist:isCategorizedBy tolx:_WorkType_painting ;
	gist:isConnectedTo told:_HistoricalPeriod_73 ;
	gist:isIdentifiedBy told:_ID_har_188 ;
	gist:isMemberOf told:_ArtCollection_rubin_museum_of_himalayan_art ;
	gist:name "Padmasambhava"@en ;
	.

told:_IntellectualProperty_52
	a gist:IntellectualProperty ;
	skos:prefLabel "The Demoness of Tibet"@en ;
	gist:description """<p>
	The Demoness of Tibet is a close copy of a well-known image located in the Tibet Museum in Lhasa. The landscape of Tibet is shown as the mythical demoness of Tibetan legend. Important Buddhist temples and monasteries are located across her body, spanning from the Ngari region in the west to Kham in the east.&nbsp;</p>"""@en ;
